The '''Jaguar XE''' (X760) is a [[compact executive car]] manufactured by [[Jaguar Land Rover]] and marketed under their [[Jaguar Cars|Jaguar marque]] from April 2015 until mid-2024 across a single generation.<ref name="AE Solihull 130415">{{cite news |last1=Allan |first1=Lawrence |last2=Burn |first2=Jonathan |url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/jaguar/90967/jaguar-begins-xe-production-at-solihull-factory |title=Jaguar begins XE production at Solihull factory |work=[[Auto Express]] |location=London |date=13 April 2015 |access-date=16 April 2015 |url-status= dead}}</ref><ref name="topgear">{{cite web |last1=Pattni |first1=Vijay |title=Jaguar has stopped building the XE, XF and F-Type; I-Pace and E-Pace to stop in Dec |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/business/jaguar-has-stopped-building-xe-xf-and-f-type-i-pace-and-e-pace-stop-dec |website=Top Gear |access-date=21 July 2024 |date=17 July 2024}}</ref> It is a four-door [[saloon car]] with a [[front-mounted engine]] and either [[rear-wheel drive]] or [[all-wheel drive]].

The successor to the [[Jaguar X-Type|X-Type]], it was designed by [[Ian Callum]] and launched at the October [[2014 Paris Motor Show]].<ref name=holloway>{{cite web |last=Holloway |first=Hilton |title=Jaguar XE confirmed as BMW 3-series rival |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/geneva-motor-show/jaguar-xe-confirmed-bmw-3-series-rival |work=Autocar |access-date=4 March 2014 |date=4 March 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150923220203/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/geneva-motor-show/jaguar-xe-confirmed-bmw-3-series-rival |archive-date=23 September 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The XE has an  [[aluminium]] suspension as well as its bonded-and-riveted [[Unibody|unitary]] aluminium  [[monocoque]] structure, without need for a reinforcing [[space frame]]. This was developed by Jaguar on their third generation [[Jaguar XJ (X350)|XJ-series (X350; from 2002)]] &mdash; the first in its segment.<ref name="AutoExpress May 2014">{{cite news |last=Rix |first=Jack |url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/car-news/87194/new-jaguar-xe-exclusive-pictures-and-details |title= New Jaguar XE: exclusive pictures and details |work=[[Auto Express]] |date=28 May 2014 |access-date=9 September 2014 }}</ref><ref name="AutoExpress Sep 2014">{{cite news |last=Fowler |first=Steve |url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/jaguar/xe/85784/new-jaguar-xe-revealed-specs-price-and-all-the-info |title= New Jaguar XE revealed: specs, price and all the info |work=[[Auto Express]] |date=8 September 2014 |access-date=9 September 2014 }}</ref>

==Launch==
The XE was announced, but not displayed, at the [[Geneva Motor Show#2014|2014 Geneva Motor Show]]<ref name=holloway/> and debuted globally on 8 September 2014 in London.<ref name="Autocar08Sep2014">{{cite news |last=Cropley |first=Steve |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/jaguar-xe-revealed-ahead-2015-launch |title=Jaguar XE revealed ahead of 2015 launch |work=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]] |date=8 September 2014 |access-date=8 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140911011941/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/jaguar-xe-revealed-ahead-2015-launch |archive-date=11 September 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Madden|first1=Luke|title=Jaguar XE tech secrets revealed|url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/jaguar/xe/87861/jaguar-xe-tech-secrets-revealed?|website=[[Auto Express]]|access-date=16 July 2014|date=15 July 2014}}</ref> Sales were expected to begin in most markets in 2015, followed by the United States in 2016.<ref>{{cite web |first=Noah |last=Joseph |url=http://www.autoblog.com/2014/03/04/2015-jaguar-xe-ingenium-official/ |title=Jaguar names its 3 Series-fighter XE, packs it with Ingenium engines |website=Autoblog |date=4 March 2014 |access-date=2017-02-19}}</ref> Production formally commenced on 13 April 2015 at Jaguar Land Rover's Solihull plant.<ref name="AE Solihull 130415" />

==Production==
The XE was the first Jaguar to be built at the factory in [[Solihull plant|Solihull]], which was to be dedicated to the manufacture of aluminium vehicles under both the Jaguar and Land Rover brands, including the second-generation [[Range Rover Sport (L494)|Range Rover Sport]].<ref name="AutoExpress Sep Interview" /> Due to high demand for the vehicle, production capacity was increased by adding a line at [[Castle Bromwich Assembly]].<ref name="ac_10160912">{{cite news|last1=Holder|first1=Jim|title=Jaguar XE production moves to Castle Bromwich|url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/industry/jaguar-xe-production-moves-castle-bromwich|access-date=4 September 2017|work=Autocar|publisher=Haymarket Media Group|date=12 September 2016}}</ref> Production transferred completely to Castle Bromwich in 2017 as part of a £100m refit.

An extended-wheelbase version—exclusively for the Chinese market—called ''XEL'' commenced production at the [[Chery Jaguar Land Rover]] plant in [[Changshu]] in 2015.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Gibbs|first1=Nick|title=Jaguar picks XE as first China-made model|url=http://europe.autonews.com/article/20141221/ANE/141219871/jaguar-picks-xe-as-first-china-made-model|website=Auto News Europe|access-date=31 December 2014|date=21 December 2014}}</ref>

==Engines==
The XE was the first application of Jaguar Land Rover's new 2.0 L [[turbocharged]] ''[[Ingenium engine family|Ingenium]]'' four-cylinder engine.<ref name=Dobie>{{cite web|last=Dobie|first=Stephen|title=A tour round the Jag XE's engine factory|url=http://www.topgear.com/uk/car-news/jaguar-ingenium-engine-factory-opens-2014-10-31|access-date=1 June 2015|date=28 January 2015|archive-date=30 June 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150630165425/http://www.topgear.com/uk/car-news/jaguar-ingenium-engine-factory-opens-2014-10-31|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Ingenium technology licenses the [[Multiair|Multiair/UniAir]] [[electrohydraulic]] variable valve lift system<ref name="autonews.com">JLR taps Schaeffler's fuel-saving system, sources say: [http://europe.autonews.com/article/20140519/ANE/140519917/jlr-taps-schaefflers-fuel-saving-valve-system-sources-say JLR taps Schaeffler's fuel-saving system, sources say], accessdate: 14. helmikuuta 2018</ref> from [[Schaeffler Group]], which Schaeffler in turn licensed from [[Fiat Chrysler Automobiles]] in 2001.<ref name="MA2">{{cite web
  | title = Schaeffler Expects Other Takers for MultiAir Technology
  | publisher = Wards Automotive
  | author = David Zoia
  | date = March 7, 2011
  | url = http://wardsauto.com/news-analysis/schaeffler-expects-other-takers-multiair-technology
  | access-date = 24 June 2020
  | archive-date = 6 August 2016
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160806050130/http://wardsauto.com/news-analysis/schaeffler-expects-other-takers-multiair-technology
  | url-status = dead
  }}</ref> The system, developed by [[Fiat Powertrain Technologies]], is a hydraulically actuated [[variable valve lift]] (VVL) technology enabling "cylinder by cylinder, stroke by stroke" control of intake air directly via an internal combustion engine's inlet valves.<ref name="MA9">{{cite web
 |title        = Fiat's Multiair engine wins Popular Science award
 |publisher    = Popular Science via Allpar.com
 |date         = November 17, 2010
 |url          = http://www.allpar.com/news/2010/11/fiats-multiair-engine-wins-popular-science-award-6549
 |archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20160303010027/http://www.allpar.com/news/2010/11/fiats-multiair-engine-wins-popular-science-award-6549
 |archive-date = 2016-03-03
 |url-status     = unfit
}}</ref>

The four-cylinder is available in both petrol and diesel variants, in a range of different power outputs. The {{cvt|163|PS|kW hp}} diesel significantly reduced consumption and carbon emission figures, and is capable of fuel consumption less than {{convert|68.9|mpgimp|L/100 km mpgus|abbr=on}} and producing CO<sub>2</sub> emissions of only 99 g/km.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.greencarguide.co.uk/car-reviews-and-road-tests/jaguar-xe-se-review/#:~:text=Jaguar%20XE%20ECONOMY%20AND%20EMISSIONS,with%20emissions%20of%20106g%2Fkm.&text=The%20manual%20XE%20model%20has,(and%2017%25%20BIK). |title=Jaguar XE SE 2.0 163 PS Review |last=Clarke |first= Paul |website=GreenCarGuide |access-date=8 March 2020}}</ref><ref name="WhatCar Sep 2014">{{cite news |last=Holder |first=Jim |url=http://www.whatcar.com/car-news/2015-jaguar-xe-revealed-full-story/1283319 |title=2015 Jaguar XE revealed - the full story |work=[[What Car]] |date=8 September 2014 |access-date=9 September 2014 |archive-date=10 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140910013841/http://www.whatcar.com/car-news/2015-jaguar-xe-revealed-full-story/1283319 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The cleanest models in Britain pay no annual [[Vehicle Excise Duty]].<ref name="AutoExpress Sep 2014" />

From the XE's launch until 2017 the petrol four-cylinders were from [[Ford Motor Company|Ford]]'s family of [[Ford EcoBoost engine#2.0 L (2010–2015)|"EcoBoost" engine]]s.<ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-review/jaguar/xe/first-drives/2015-jaguar-xe-20i-240ps-portfolio-review |title=2015 Jaguar XE 2.0i 240PS Portfolio review |last=Burt |first=Matt |website=Autocar |date=30 April 2015 |access-date=13 March 2020 |quote=Of course, as with Land Rover Discovery Sports using old-school diesel engines while they wait for the shiny Ingenium units that are such stars in the XE, so the Jag’s petrol engines aren’t really brewed up in the firm’s shiny new plant in Wolverhampton. They’re Ford units, made in Valencia; Ingenium petrols are a further year away, allegedly.}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url=https://europe.autonews.com/article/20160916/ANE/160919894/jaguar-land-rover-drops-ford-engine-as-it-expands-ingenium-family |title=Jaguar Land Rover drops Ford engine as it expands Ingenium family |last=Gibbs |first=Nick |date=16 September 2016 |access-date=13 March 2020 |website=Automotive News Europe}}</ref> New Ingenium petrol engines replaced the Ford units beginning in the 2018 model year.<ref name="enginetechintl">{{cite web| url=https://www.enginetechnologyinternational.com/news/new-engine/jaguar-unveils-revised-ingenium-engines-for-f-pace-xf-and-xe.html |title=Jaguar unveils revised Ingenium engines for F-Pace, XF and XE |last=Slavnich |first=Dean |date=15 February 2017 |access-date=8 March 2020}}</ref>

From the XE's launch until 2019 the petrol V6 was from [[Jaguar Cars|Jaguar]]'s family of [[Jaguar AJ-V8 engine|AJ-V8 engine]]s.<ref name=ebbs>{{cite web|last=Ebbs|first=Chris|title=New Jaguar saloon to be called XE|url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/jaguar/86011/new-jaguar-saloon-be-called-xe|work=Auto Express|access-date=4 March 2014|date=4 March 2014}}</ref><ref name="TopGear Sep 14">{{cite news |last=Pattni |first=Vijay |url=http://www.topgear.com/uk/car-news/new-jaguar-xe-revealed-2014-9-8 |title=Welcome to the new Jaguar XE |work=[[Top Gear (magazine)|Top Gear]] |date=8 September 2014 |access-date=9 September 2014 |archive-date=30 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150630165423/http://www.topgear.com/uk/car-news/new-jaguar-xe-revealed-2014-9-8 |url-status=dead }}</ref> After 2019, the V6 engine was no longer offered.<ref name="C&D 2020">{{cite web| title=The Updated Jaguar XE Deserves a Better Engine |last1=Siler |first1=Steve |last2=Sutton |first2=Mike |date=29 January 2020 |access-date=7 March 2020 |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/reviews/a27226102/2020-jaguar-xe-first-drive/ |website=Car & Driver |publisher=Hearst Autos, Inc.}}</ref>

In late 2020, [[mild hybrid]] options were announced, as modifications to the Ingenium 2.0L engines with an additional [[48-volt electrical system|48 Volt]] [[BAS hybrid|belt alternator starter]] system.<ref>{{Cite web |title=JAGUAR XE: UPDATED WITH NEW CONNECTED TECHNOLOGIES AND MILD-HYBRID POWER {{!}} JLR Corporate Website |url=https://www.jaguarlandrover.com/news/2020/10/jaguar-xe-updated-new-connected-technologies-and-mild-hybrid-power |access-date=2024-09-25 |website=www.jaguarlandrover.com}}</ref>

